    "description": "Industrial chemicals connect mineral processing and chemical manufacturing with industries that need acids, alkalis, solvents, and other process inputs. Iranian supplier descriptions document a varied product offering, but they do not establish comprehensive national production totals or independently verified environmental performance.",
    "tags": ["Industrial chemicals", "Iranian industry", "Chlor-alkali", "Soda ash", "Sulfuric acid", "Mineral processing"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:08:35Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:08:35Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Industrial Chemicals in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore industrial acids, alkalis, and chemical inputs in Iran, their manufacturing relationships, and the limits of available production evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The inputs behind other industries",
        "content": "Industrial chemicals are often encountered through what they help another factory make. Sulfuric acid can serve mineral processing; soda ash enters glass manufacture; chlorine chemistry supplies intermediates and treatment chemicals. These connections explain why this subject sits between mining, basic manufacturing, and the more recognizable worlds of fertilizers, polymers, and finished consumer products. The boundaries overlap: a substance can be an industrial input in one transaction and a finished chemical product in another. [s2][s3][s4]\n\nIranian commercial descriptions illustrate that range. Sepehran Chemistry lists sodium bicarbonate, caustic soda, solvents, glycols, and carbon dioxide among its offerings. It describes itself as both a manufacturer and a trader. Its catalogue therefore establishes the company's advertised portfolio, rather than proving domestic manufacture of every listed substance or measuring the national industry. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "chemical-families",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Acids and alkalis have different industrial roles",
        "content": "Caustic soda is sodium hydroxide. In chlor-alkali production, an electric current passes through a salt solution to produce chlorine, hydrogen, and sodium hydroxide. The products must be kept appropriately separated during processing. This is a linked manufacturing system: describing chlorine production without its accompanying alkali and hydrogen gives an incomplete account of the process. The EPA reference explains the chemistry and historical cell technologies; it does not identify which technologies Iranian factories operate today. [s2]\n\nSoda ash is sodium carbonate, a different compound from caustic soda. Its uses include glass, chemicals, soaps, and detergents. It can be manufactured through synthetic routes or recovered from suitable natural materials. The existence of those alternatives internationally does not establish the production route at an individual Iranian plant. [s3]\n\nSulfuric acid provides another connection to mineral industries. Its applications include phosphate-fertilizer manufacture and copper leaching. The contact process converts sulfur-bearing feed into sulfur dioxide, then sulfur trioxide, before absorption produces additional acid. Feedstocks can include elemental sulfur and appropriate metallurgical gas streams. These are general process relationships, rather than a verified inventory of Iranian installations. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "chlor-alkali-relationship",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Why chlor-alkali products belong together",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Salt solution\"] -->|\"Feed preparation\"| B[\"Prepared brine\"]\n  B -->|\"Electrolysis using direct current\"| C[\"Chlor-alkali process\"]\n  C -->|\"Separated product\"| D[\"Chlorine\"]\n  C -->|\"Separated product\"| E[\"Sodium hydroxide solution\"]\n  C -->|\"Separated product\"| F[\"Hydrogen\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows show material preparation and the linked products of chlor-alkali electrolysis. This is a general chemical-process diagram, not a map of an Iranian facility or its current equipment.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iranian-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A supplier example beyond petrochemicals",
        "content": "Tehran Sulphur, identifying its legal company name as Kimia Javid Tehran, advertises sodium sulfide for mining, leather, paper, and textile applications. Its Persian description places the factory in Jannatabad Industrial Estate on the Khavaran road outside Tehran. Sodium sulfide should not be confused with sodium sulfate or elemental sulfur merely because their English names resemble one another. Here, the company's product identification supports the distinction. [s6]\n\nThe same website claims improvements in production and pollution performance. Those statements remain company claims: no independent emissions measurements were inspected to establish their magnitude or effectiveness. Similarly, advertised capacity is not evidence of annual realized output. [s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evidence-and-conclusion",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A useful industry map needs product-level evidence",
        "content": "USGS explicitly identifies gaps in reliable Iranian output estimates for chemicals including caustic soda and soda ash. Missing national figures should therefore remain missing rather than being reconstructed from supplier advertisements. [s1]\n\nThe clearest supported picture is an interconnected supply system, with distinct chemical products serving different industrial customers. Establishing its scale requires comparable product-level production records; assessing its environmental performance requires facility-specific evidence. The sources inspected support the chemical relationships and selected commercial examples, but further research is needed for a publication-ready account of Iran's principal producers, regional distribution, and recent operating conditions. [s1][s2][s3][s4][s5][s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4", "s5", "s6"]
      }
    ],
