  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-17-4-1",
    "page_path": "/explore/mining/building-materials/cement/",
    "name": "Cement",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/mining/building-materials/",
    "entity_type": "category",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran's cement industry transforms mineral raw materials into the binder used in concrete and mortar. USGS records a fluctuating production path, while newer estimates and separate clinker statistics require careful treatment of reporting vintages and product definitions.",
    "tags": ["Cement", "Clinker", "Iranian industry", "Construction materials", "Industrial energy", "Production statistics"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:08:35Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:08:35Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Cement Production in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran's cement industry, its mineral-to-clinker process, reported annual production, newer estimates, and energy and emissions distinctions."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The binder behind concrete",
        "content": "Cement is the fine binder that reacts with water and holds aggregates together in concrete. Concrete is the resulting composite material, rather than another name for cement. That distinction is essential when interpreting an industry whose output is frequently discussed alongside housing, roads, and other construction. A cement-production figure measures the manufactured binder, not the quantity of concrete placed or the number of buildings completed. [s4]\n\nIran has a substantial cement industry. The USGS country report records finished cement separately from clinker and identifies both domestic consumption and exports. Its account of 2024 names Iraq, Kuwait, Afghanistan, Russia, and Pakistan among destinations for combined cement-and-clinker exports. Those combined shipments should not be described as exports of finished cement alone. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "manufacturing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why clinker matters",
        "content": "The manufacturing sequence starts with preparing and blending mineral raw materials. High-temperature processing creates clinker, the hard intermediate material that is cooled and then ground with gypsum and other appropriate ingredients to produce cement. Gypsum helps control setting behavior. An integrated plant therefore performs a different range of operations from a works that only grinds purchased clinker. [s3]\n\nRaw-material chemistry influences the blend and the process. Calcium-bearing materials such as limestone are combined with sources of silicon, aluminum, and iron. During processing, limestone decomposition releases carbon dioxide; fuel combustion provides another source of emissions. Cleaner electricity alone cannot eliminate the carbon dioxide released by the mineral reaction. This is a chemical distinction, not an estimate of emissions at any particular Iranian plant. [s3][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "manufacturing-flow",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "From mineral inputs to cement",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Mineral raw materials\"] -->|\"Crushing, grinding, and blending\"| B[\"Prepared raw mix\"]\n  B -->|\"High-temperature processing\"| C[\"Clinker\"]\n  C -->|\"Cooling\"| D[\"Cooled clinker\"]\n  D -->|\"Finish grinding\"| E[\"Cement\"]\n  F[\"Gypsum and suitable additions\"] -->|\"Added during finishing\"| E\n```",
        "summary": "The diagram separates clinker production from cement finishing. It shows the general material sequence, without implying that every Iranian works carries out every stage.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "production-trend",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A fluctuating production path",
        "content": "The historical series below retains the observations and revision flags in a single USGS country-report table. It shows growth followed by a setback and recovery, rather than uninterrupted expansion. The final observation is the highest in this particular five-year window. Output alone does not establish profitability, construction demand, or the cause of any annual change. [s1]\n\nA newer USGS summary supplies estimates through 2025. These appear in a separate dataset because they are a different statistical release with rounded estimates. The country report's more detailed 2024 figure and the summary's rounded estimate are not identical; neither has been silently substituted into the historical chart. [s2][s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s6"]
      },
